  "verbalization": "Certonardosterol E3 is shown to have a target relationship with HepG2 through multiple pathways, with the core mechanism involving the inhibition of microtubule-associated proteins and the activation of dehydrogenase/reductase enzymes. The evidence suggests that Certonardosterol E3 may act through two distinct pathways: one targeting Microtubule-associated protein 1B, which in turn influences HepG2, and another targeting Dehydrogenase/reductase SDR family member 9, which ultimately leads to HepG2. These pathways are supported by high scores in the final score calculation, indicating a strong and consistent relationship between Certonardosterol E3 and HepG2. The overlapping targets and the shared downstream effect on HepG2 suggest a potential mechanism involving cellular metabolic or structural disruption, which could be relevant in the context of cancer treatment. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of the relationship, with the core mechanism likely involving the modulation of microtubule dynamics or enzymatic activity, which are critical for cell survival and proliferation in HepG2 cells.",
